    "brute": "N/A — DFS/BFS with a visited map is the standard, necessary approach given the graph may contain cycles.",
    "optimal": "DFS (or BFS) from the given node, using a hash map from original node → cloned node. Before recursing into a neighbor, check if it's already been cloned (present in the map); if so, reuse that clone instead of recursing again.",
    "intuition": "Because the graph can have cycles, a naive traversal would recurse forever — the visited map both prevents infinite recursion AND lets you correctly wire up clones that reference each other in a cycle.",
    "dryRun": "Start node1. map={}. Clone node1 → map={1:clone1}. Visit neighbors 2,4. Clone2 not in map → clone it, map={1:c1,2:c2}, recurse into 2's neighbors(1,3): 1 already in map → reuse c1. Clone3 → map adds 3:c3, recurse into 3's neighbors(2,4): 2 in map → reuse. Continue until all nodes cloned and wired.",
